{
  "analysis": [
    {
      "layout": "P",
      "strengths": "All four columns are visible at once, making it possible to follow a class across the row without scrolling. Its wider Behavior column produces shorter rows than R and S.",
      "faults": "Nine measured breaks inside words interrupt both labels and examples; PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR, punctuation, and dictionaries are particularly conspicuous. The split Descriptive Name header weakens the table’s first point of orientation. Long, uneven rows make vertical comparison laborious.",
      "spatial_logic": "The class column gets its minimum useful width, while the remaining space favors Behavior slightly. That choice acknowledges the length of the explanations but leaves too little room for long words in the two middle columns."
    },
    {
      "layout": "Q",
      "strengths": "No measured words break internally. Class, name, and examples are visible initially, and the scroll distance is modest compared with T and U. Its height is substantially lower than the fitting layouts.",
      "faults": "The Behavior column begins in the initial viewport but most of its text requires scrolling. Narrow names and examples still create many short lines, and the 60 px header and irregular tall rows make the table feel dense.",
      "spatial_logic": "Q spends just enough width on the middle columns to preserve long words, then gives the larger share to Behavior. It trades a small horizontal overflow for a marked improvement in reading continuity."
    },
    {
      "layout": "R",
      "strengths": "All columns are visible together, and the wider name column preserves some words that P splits. Its seven measured internal word breaks are fewer than P’s nine.",
      "faults": "The header and several examples still split inside words. The narrow Behavior column creates an eight-line cell and a very tall table, with large blank areas beside long explanations.",
      "spatial_logic": "R distributes the available width more evenly among the three text columns. This helps some labels but takes space from the consistently prose-heavy Behavior column."
    },
    {
      "layout": "S",
      "strengths": "Like R, it shows the complete row without horizontal scrolling and has fewer measured internal word breaks than P.",
      "faults": "It retains the split header and broken example words, and its height and row-height pattern match R. The one-pixel shifts do not produce a meaningful improvement visible in this render.",
      "spatial_logic": "S is essentially R with one pixel moved from the name column to Behavior. It tests a nearly identical balance between the three text columns."
    },
    {
      "layout": "T",
      "strengths": "The most compact vertically, with intact words, single-line headers, and at most three lines per cell. Names and examples read naturally, and the generous Behavior width supports comparison of explanations.",
      "faults": "Behavior is entirely beyond the initial viewport, and the large scroll distance separates it from the class identifier during lookup. Some rows contain considerable empty space where a neighboring explanation determines their height.",
      "spatial_logic": "T gives the name and example columns enough room for comfortable phrases and assigns the largest share to Behavior. It prioritizes fluent cells and compact rows over seeing the whole relationship at once."
    },
    {
      "layout": "U",
      "strengths": "Like T, it preserves words and single-line headers while keeping cells to at most three lines. It requires slightly less horizontal scrolling and presents the first three columns clearly on arrival.",
      "faults": "Behavior remains offscreen initially. The narrower Behavior column increases several row heights, making U taller than T without restoring cross-column visibility.",
      "spatial_logic": "U trims T’s name and Behavior widths while retaining the generous example column. The savings reduce horizontal travel somewhat, at the cost of more wrapping in explanatory rows."
    }
  ],
  "insights": "The main tradeoff is between seeing a complete row at once and preserving readable words and phrases. At 359 px, the minimum widths needed to preserve the longest units exceed the available space, so a fitting design must accept some internal breaks. Q is the strongest scrolling compromise for relating identifiers to behavior; T is stronger for fluent reading and compact vertical scanning, but requires much more horizontal travel. P is the strongest fitting candidate for overall density, although its broken words remain a serious fault. The proposed widths are hypotheses: their exact wrapping and viewport behavior need rendered verification.",
